================================================================================================
EXACT INTERVAL vs THE FIRST-ORDER ONE — is the symmetry an artefact of O(theta)?
================================================================================================
  n=3, tau=0.35, real Heisenberg, Y = sum_i X_i (so (A) holds),
  pure branch (so (B) holds). Theorem 2 therefore predicts a SYMMETRIC
  interval at first order. Exact endpoints found by optimisation over U(8).

  spectral bounds: W_floor -0.10275, W_ceil 2.89725  (span 3.00000 = lam_max - lam_min)
  'sat' columns are how far each endpoint has run into its bound. Any row
  with sat above ~20% is reporting the spectrum, not the protocol.

     theta      W_min      W_max   sat-   sat+      asym   fo_endpt asym/theta
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.003   -0.00230    0.00232   2.2%   0.1%    0.0053    0.00231     2.1369
     0.005   -0.00457    0.00467   4.4%   0.2%    0.0107    0.00462     2.1372
     0.010   -0.00905    0.00944   8.8%   0.3%    0.0214    0.00924     2.1384
     0.020   -0.01770    0.01928  17.2%   0.7%    0.0429    0.01849     2.1433
     0.040   -0.03381    0.04022  32.9%   1.4%    0.0866    0.03697     2.1649

  (2) HOW BIG IS THE DIRECTED PART, and does it scale as O(theta^2)?
      NOT compared against the paper's both-conditions-broken interval:
      that uses a rank-2 projector generator whose spectral range differs
      from sum_i X_i, so the magnitudes are not commensurable. An earlier
      version of this file made that comparison and reported multipliers
      up to 25000x. Those were saturation against an incommensurable
      baseline and are withdrawn. What IS comparable is the SCALING.
     theta  directed |W_max+W_min|  asym/theta    usable?
  -------------------------------------------------------
     0.0025               0.000025      2.1369        yes
     0.0050               0.000099      2.1372        yes
     0.0100               0.000395      2.1384        yes
     0.0200               0.001585      2.1433        yes
     0.0400               0.006411      2.1649  SATURATED

  asym/theta over the UNSATURATED rows: 2.1369, 2.1372, 2.1384, 2.1433
  spread 0.0064 over a 8x range of theta.
  A constant here is the signature of a genuine O(theta^2) directed
  term: asym = (W_2/W_1) theta exactly. Saturation or an optimiser
  artefact would not hold a constant across that range.

  If asym stays ~0 across theta, the first-order picture is operational and
  the classification stands. If asym grows and the directed part exceeds
  the paper's own both-conditions-broken result, then finite theta supplies
  directed work WITHOUT breaking (A) or (B), and Sec. 'Two independent
  causes' needs a scope line saying so - the conditions govern the
  linearised protocol, not the protocol.
